WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
In this work, we present
how to guarantee human

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.920
safety in human-robot
collaboration from camera images.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.460
If our prediction is wrong,
we speak of a failure.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.180
This failure is dangerous if the robot

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.340
would actually touch the
human in this instance.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.300
The ISO standard requires safety
systems to have a probably

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.260
of dangerous failures per hour
of less than one in a million,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.680
which is at most one dangerous
failure every 114 years.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.680
To achieve this,
we propose the following system.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.780
First, our 2D pose estimation predicts the
joint positions and covariance matrices.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.180
Our uncertainty-aware triangulation lifts the

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.720
poses and covariances into three dimensions.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.060
We then input two seconds of pose
history in our motion prediction transformer

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.420
to predict the future poses and their
covariances in the next 400 milliseconds.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.320
To establish strong probabilistic guarantees,

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.840
we use conformal calibration:

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.220
We set the score threshold to
the 99.99th percentile that

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:03.100
scales every covariance matrix
into a conformal prediction set.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.620
The spheres now become capsules,
which define the human's reachable occupancy.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.300
SARA shield intersects them with
the robot's reachable occupancy.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.700
If there is no intersection,
we execute the trajectory.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.540
Otherwise, a failsafe stop.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.860
To handle rare OOD inputs,
we detect them with gradient-based

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.940
monitors and reuse the previous
prediction to resume operation.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:46.820
Our experimental results on the human
3.6 million dataset show that our

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.880
reachable occupancies in blue have a
seven point six times smaller volume

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.700
than the constant-velocity model of ISO
thirteen eight fifty-five in orange.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.520
To determine the probability of dangerous
failures per hour of our system,

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.180
we take every prediction and
randomly place it within

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.360
a ten-meter circle around the
robot 265 billion times.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.660
Almost none of those
placements can reach the robot;

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.540
hence we pre-filter them and evaluate
the remaining motions on the GPU.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.420
For our system, four of
those placements ended in

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.300
contact although the shield
had verified the trajectory.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:25.800
Using a Clopper-Pearson bound,

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.280
we can say with 99.999 percent
confidence that our system has a

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.460
dangerous failure rate below the required
1 in a million failures per hour.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.080
In our hardware experiments
we evaluate our pipeline on

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.200
a Franka arm and a RealSense
camera at twenty-five hertz.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.880
Blue is the human's conformal prediction
sets over the robot's stopping horizon.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.260
When they intersect the robot's
reachable set, the shield brakes.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.540
In every tested instance the robot came to a

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.800
complete stop before the human could reach it.

